WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.554
Our heart pumps blood through an extensive
network of blood vessels.

00:00:05.043 --> 00:00:08.231
This is how oxygen and nutrients reach our cells.

00:00:08.746 --> 00:00:13.212
At the same time, blood collects waste products
and carries them away.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:16.505
Arteries carry blood away from the heart.

00:00:16.956 --> 00:00:21.548
In tiny capillaries, substances are exchanged
between blood and tissue.

00:00:21.994 --> 00:00:24.752
Veins bring blood back to the heart.

00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:28.471
Red and blue help us distinguish blood vessels.

00:00:28.911 --> 00:00:30.942
But our blood is always red.

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:35.614
Endovascular means: inside a blood vessel.

00:00:36.308 --> 00:00:39.254
A catheter is a thin, flexible tube.

00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:44.441
Doctors carefully guide it through the inside of
a blood vessel.

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:48.343
They track its position using medical imaging.

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:51.643
We show one possible route to the heart.

00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:54.964
The catheter starts in a vein in the groin.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.684
It advances through the pelvic veins into the
inferior vena cava.

00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:05.162
This large vein returns blood from the lower body
to the heart.

00:01:05.596 --> 00:01:09.258
From there, the catheter tip reaches the right
atrium.

00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:12.169
That is one of the heart’s two upper chambers.

00:01:12.643 --> 00:01:16.444
Depending on the examination, a catheter can
measure pressure, for example.

00:01:16.896 --> 00:01:21.421
Other procedures use arteries and therefore
follow a different route.

00:01:22.026 --> 00:01:26.541
At our station, you explore this principle using
a simplified model.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.220
You control it from outside and watch the tip.

00:01:30.746 --> 00:01:36.309
The challenge is to find the right route and
control your movements precisely.
